**Key idea:** Mirroring a tree = swap the two children of every node; the order you visit nodes in doesn't matter.

```tree
[4,7*,2*,9,6,3,1]
caption: output: children of every node swapped
```

**Brute force:** Build a brand-new mirrored copy (new node per node, `copy.left = mirror(old.right)`). Correct, but allocates a second tree. Time O(n), Space O(n).

:::details 1. DFS (recursive)
Invert the right subtree and hang it on the left, invert the old left subtree and hang it on the right. The base case is an empty subtree. Post-order: children are finished before the parent's pointers change, so nothing is lost.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode invertTree(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return null;
        TreeNode left = invertTree(root.left);
        root.left = invertTree(root.right);
        root.right = left;
        return root;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def invertTree(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:
        if not root:
            return None
        root.left, root.right = self.invertTree(root.right), self.invertTree(root.left)
        return root
```

**Time:** O(n), every node visited once
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack; O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed
:::

:::details 2. BFS (queue, level by level)
Push the root into a queue. Pop a node, swap its children, push any non-null children. Each node is swapped exactly once, and a level is finished before the next one starts.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode invertTree(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return null;
        Deque<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root);
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = queue.poll();
            TreeNode tmp = node.left;
            node.left = node.right;
            node.right = tmp;
            if (node.left != null) queue.offer(node.left);
            if (node.right != null) queue.offer(node.right);
        }
        return root;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def invertTree(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:
        if not root:
            return None
        queue = deque([root])
        while queue:
            node = queue.popleft()
            node.left, node.right = node.right, node.left
            if node.left:
                queue.append(node.left)
            if node.right:
                queue.append(node.right)
        return root
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(w), where w is the widest level (up to about n/2 in a full tree)
:::

:::details 3. Iterative DFS (explicit stack)
Same as BFS, but with a stack instead of a queue. This avoids recursion-depth limits on a very skewed tree, which matters in Python.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode invertTree(TreeNode root) {
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        if (root != null) stack.push(root);
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = stack.pop();
            TreeNode tmp = node.left;
            node.left = node.right;
            node.right = tmp;
            if (node.left != null) stack.push(node.left);
            if (node.right != null) stack.push(node.right);
        }
        return root;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def invertTree(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:
        stack = [root] if root else []
        while stack:
            node = stack.pop()
            node.left, node.right = node.right, node.left
            if node.left:
                stack.append(node.left)
            if node.right:
                stack.append(node.right)
        return root
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(h) for the stack
:::

**Pitfall:** Swapping with `root.left = invertTree(root.right); root.right = invertTree(root.left)` without saving the old left first. The second call then sees the already-replaced child.

**Key idea:** The depth of a tree is 1 (for the root) plus the deeper of its two subtrees; an empty subtree has depth 0.

**Brute force:** List every root-to-leaf path explicitly (copying the path at each leaf) and take the longest. Time O(n·h) for the copies, Space O(n·h) if you keep them all.

:::details 1. DFS (recursive)
Ask each child for its depth, take the larger answer and add one for the current node. Null returns 0, so a leaf comes back as 1. Every node is asked exactly once.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return 0;
        return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(root.left), maxDepth(root.right));
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def maxDepth(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:
        if not root:
            return 0
        return 1 + max(self.maxDepth(root.left), self.maxDepth(root.right))
```

**Time:** O(n), each node visited once
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack; O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed
:::

:::details 2. BFS (count the levels)
Walk the tree level by level with a queue. Snapshot the queue size, pop exactly that many nodes and push their children: that is one full level. The number of levels processed is the depth.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return 0;
        Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        q.add(root);
        int depth = 0;
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            depth++;
            for (int i = q.size(); i > 0; i--) {
                TreeNode node = q.poll();
                if (node.left != null) q.add(node.left);
                if (node.right != null) q.add(node.right);
            }
        }
        return depth;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def maxDepth(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:
        if not root:
            return 0
        q, depth = deque([root]), 0
        while q:
            depth += 1
            for _ in range(len(q)):
                node = q.popleft()
                if node.left:
                    q.append(node.left)
                if node.right:
                    q.append(node.right)
        return depth
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(w), w = widest level (up to about n/2)
:::

:::details 3. Iterative DFS (stack of node + depth)
Push `(node, depth)` pairs onto a stack. Each time you pop a real node, update the best depth seen and push its children with `depth + 1`. Same answer as recursion without the call-stack limit.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
        Deque<Object[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        if (root != null) stack.push(new Object[]{root, 1});
        int best = 0;
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            Object[] top = stack.pop();
            TreeNode node = (TreeNode) top[0];
            int d = (int) top[1];
            best = Math.max(best, d);
            if (node.left != null) stack.push(new Object[]{node.left, d + 1});
            if (node.right != null) stack.push(new Object[]{node.right, d + 1});
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def maxDepth(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:
        stack, best = [(root, 1)], 0
        while stack:
            node, d = stack.pop()
            if node:
                best = max(best, d)
                stack.append((node.left, d + 1))
                stack.append((node.right, d + 1))
        return best
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(h) for the stack
:::

**Pitfall:** Returning 1 for a null node counts an extra level under every leaf.

**Key idea:** Every path has one highest node. At that node the path length is height(left) + height(right), so compute heights bottom-up and track the best sum.

**Brute force:** For every node, compute the height of its left and right subtree from scratch and take the max of left + right over all nodes (shown as approach 3). Time O(n²) on a skewed tree, Space O(h).

:::details 1. DFS returning height (optimal)
One post-order pass. `height(node)` returns 1 + the taller child, and on the way it updates a global best with `left + right`, the longest path that bends at this node. Each node is processed once.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int best = 0;

    public int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode root) {
        height(root);
        return best;
    }

    private int height(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;
        int l = height(node.left), r = height(node.right);
        best = Math.max(best, l + r);
        return 1 + Math.max(l, r);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def diameterOfBinaryTree(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:
        best = 0

        def height(node):
            nonlocal best
            if not node:
                return 0
            l, r = height(node.left), height(node.right)
            best = max(best, l + r)
            return 1 + max(l, r)

        height(root)
        return best
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

:::details 2. Iterative post-order with a height map
Simulate the recursion with an explicit stack: keep a node on the stack until both children have a height in the map, then pop it, compute its height and update the best. Useful when the tree is deep enough to overflow the call stack.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode root) {
        Map<TreeNode, Integer> height = new HashMap<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        if (root != null) stack.push(root);
        int best = 0;
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = stack.peek();
            if (node.left != null && !height.containsKey(node.left)) {
                stack.push(node.left);
            } else if (node.right != null && !height.containsKey(node.right)) {
                stack.push(node.right);
            } else {
                stack.pop();
                int l = height.getOrDefault(node.left, 0), r = height.getOrDefault(node.right, 0);
                best = Math.max(best, l + r);
                height.put(node, 1 + Math.max(l, r));
            }
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def diameterOfBinaryTree(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:
        height = {None: 0}
        best = 0
        stack = [root] if root else []
        while stack:
            node = stack[-1]
            if node.left and node.left not in height:
                stack.append(node.left)
            elif node.right and node.right not in height:
                stack.append(node.right)
            else:
                stack.pop()
                l, r = height[node.left], height[node.right]
                best = max(best, l + r)
                height[node] = 1 + max(l, r)
        return best
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(n) for the map, O(h) for the stack
:::

:::details 3. Brute force: height from scratch at every node
The diameter is either the path through the root (height(left) + height(right)) or lies fully inside one subtree, so recurse on both. Simple, but heights are recomputed for every ancestor.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return 0;
        int throughRoot = height(root.left) + height(root.right);
        return Math.max(throughRoot, Math.max(diameterOfBinaryTree(root.left), diameterOfBinaryTree(root.right)));
    }

    private int height(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;
        return 1 + Math.max(height(node.left), height(node.right));
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def diameterOfBinaryTree(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:
        def height(node):
            if not node:
                return 0
            return 1 + max(height(node.left), height(node.right))

        if not root:
            return 0
        through_root = height(root.left) + height(root.right)
        return max(through_root,
                   self.diameterOfBinaryTree(root.left),
                   self.diameterOfBinaryTree(root.right))
```

**Time:** O(n²) worst case (skewed), O(n log n) balanced
**Space:** O(h)
:::

**Pitfall:** Counting nodes instead of edges. With heights defined as "nodes on the longest downward path", `left + right` is already the edge count; don't add 1.

**Key idea:** Compute heights bottom-up and check the balance condition in the same pass; signal "unbalanced" with a sentinel (−1) so the check costs nothing extra.

**Brute force:** At every node compute both subtree heights from scratch and recurse into the children (approach 3). Time O(n²) skewed, Space O(h).

:::details 1. DFS with early exit (optimal)
`height(node)` returns the subtree height, or −1 as soon as any subtree below is unbalanced. A parent that sees −1 just passes it up, so the first violation short-circuits the rest of the work.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isBalanced(TreeNode root) {
        return height(root) >= 0;
    }

    // height of the subtree, or -1 if it is unbalanced
    private int height(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;
        int l = height(node.left);
        if (l < 0) return -1;
        int r = height(node.right);
        if (r < 0 || Math.abs(l - r) > 1) return -1;
        return 1 + Math.max(l, r);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isBalanced(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        def height(node):
            # height of the subtree, or -1 if it is unbalanced
            if not node:
                return 0
            l = height(node.left)
            if l < 0:
                return -1
            r = height(node.right)
            if r < 0 or abs(l - r) > 1:
                return -1
            return 1 + max(l, r)

        return height(root) >= 0
```

**Time:** O(n), each node visited once
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

:::details 2. Iterative post-order
Keep a node on an explicit stack until both children have known heights, then pop it, compare the two heights and record its own. Avoids recursion depth limits on a 5000-node chain.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isBalanced(TreeNode root) {
        Map<TreeNode, Integer> height = new HashMap<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        if (root != null) stack.push(root);
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = stack.peek();
            if (node.left != null && !height.containsKey(node.left)) {
                stack.push(node.left);
            } else if (node.right != null && !height.containsKey(node.right)) {
                stack.push(node.right);
            } else {
                stack.pop();
                int l = height.getOrDefault(node.left, 0), r = height.getOrDefault(node.right, 0);
                if (Math.abs(l - r) > 1) return false;
                height.put(node, 1 + Math.max(l, r));
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isBalanced(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        height = {None: 0}
        stack = [root] if root else []
        while stack:
            node = stack[-1]
            if node.left and node.left not in height:
                stack.append(node.left)
            elif node.right and node.right not in height:
                stack.append(node.right)
            else:
                stack.pop()
                l, r = height[node.left], height[node.right]
                if abs(l - r) > 1:
                    return False
                height[node] = 1 + max(l, r)
        return True
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(n) for the height map
:::

:::details 3. Top-down (recompute heights)
Check the root's two heights, then check each subtree recursively. Correct and short, but a node's height is recomputed once for every ancestor.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isBalanced(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return true;
        if (Math.abs(height(root.left) - height(root.right)) > 1) return false;
        return isBalanced(root.left) && isBalanced(root.right);
    }

    private int height(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;
        return 1 + Math.max(height(node.left), height(node.right));
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isBalanced(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        def height(node):
            if not node:
                return 0
            return 1 + max(height(node.left), height(node.right))

        if not root:
            return True
        if abs(height(root.left) - height(root.right)) > 1:
            return False
        return self.isBalanced(root.left) and self.isBalanced(root.right)
```

**Time:** O(n²) worst case, O(n log n) balanced
**Space:** O(h)
:::

**Pitfall:** Checking only the root. A tree can have equal-height subtrees at the root and still be unbalanced deeper down (`[1,2,2,3,null,null,3,4,null,null,4]` is exactly that shape).

**Key idea:** Walk both trees in lockstep. Two empty nodes match; one empty or different values is a mismatch; otherwise both pairs of children must match.

**Brute force:** Serialize both trees (with explicit null markers so shape is captured) and compare the strings. Time O(n + m), Space O(n + m).

:::details 1. DFS (recursive)
Compare the current pair, then recurse into (left, left) and (right, right). `&&` stops at the first mismatch.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        if (p == null && q == null) return true;
        if (p == null || q == null || p.val != q.val) return false;
        return isSameTree(p.left, q.left) && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isSameTree(self, p: Optional[TreeNode], q: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        if not p and not q:
            return True
        if not p or not q or p.val != q.val:
            return False
        return self.isSameTree(p.left, q.left) and self.isSameTree(p.right, q.right)
```

**Time:** O(min(n, m)), stops at the first difference
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

:::details 2. BFS (queue of pairs)
Push the pair `(p, q)` into a queue. Pop a pair, skip it if both are null, fail if exactly one is null or the values differ, else push the two child pairs.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        Deque<TreeNode[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.add(new TreeNode[]{p, q});
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode[] pair = queue.poll();
            TreeNode a = pair[0], b = pair[1];
            if (a == null && b == null) continue;
            if (a == null || b == null || a.val != b.val) return false;
            queue.add(new TreeNode[]{a.left, b.left});
            queue.add(new TreeNode[]{a.right, b.right});
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isSameTree(self, p: Optional[TreeNode], q: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        queue = deque([(p, q)])
        while queue:
            a, b = queue.popleft()
            if not a and not b:
                continue
            if not a or not b or a.val != b.val:
                return False
            queue.append((a.left, b.left))
            queue.append((a.right, b.right))
        return True
```

**Time:** O(min(n, m))
**Space:** O(w), the widest level
:::

**Pitfall:** Comparing only values (e.g. traversal lists without null markers). `[1,2]` and `[1,null,2]` have the same pre-order values but different shapes.

**Key idea:** Try every node of `root` as a starting point and run a Same Tree check against `subRoot`; or serialize both trees and turn it into a substring search.

**Brute force:** Approach 1 is the natural brute force: a full Same Tree comparison at each of the n nodes. Time O(n·m), Space O(h).

:::details 1. DFS + Same Tree at every node
Walk `root`. At each node, call `same(node, subRoot)`; if it fails, try the left and right subtrees. The comparison usually fails fast, so it runs well in practice.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSubtree(TreeNode root, TreeNode subRoot) {
        if (subRoot == null) return true;
        if (root == null) return false;
        if (same(root, subRoot)) return true;
        return isSubtree(root.left, subRoot) || isSubtree(root.right, subRoot);
    }

    private boolean same(TreeNode a, TreeNode b) {
        if (a == null && b == null) return true;
        if (a == null || b == null || a.val != b.val) return false;
        return same(a.left, b.left) && same(a.right, b.right);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isSubtree(self, root: Optional[TreeNode], subRoot: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        if not subRoot:
            return True
        if not root:
            return False
        if self.same(root, subRoot):
            return True
        return self.isSubtree(root.left, subRoot) or self.isSubtree(root.right, subRoot)

    def same(self, a, b):
        if not a and not b:
            return True
        if not a or not b or a.val != b.val:
            return False
        return self.same(a.left, b.left) and self.same(a.right, b.right)
```

**Time:** O(n·m) worst case, n and m the two tree sizes
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

:::details 2. Serialize + string matching (KMP)
Write both trees in pre-order with a null marker (`#`) and a marker before every value (`^`). A subtree of `root` is a contiguous block in that string, so `subRoot` is a subtree exactly when its string occurs inside `root`'s. Find it with KMP in linear time. The `^` marker stops `2` from matching the tail of `12`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSubtree(TreeNode root, TreeNode subRoot) {
        String text = encode(root, new StringBuilder()).toString();
        String pat = encode(subRoot, new StringBuilder()).toString();
        int[] lps = new int[pat.length()];
        for (int i = 1, k = 0; i < pat.length(); i++) {
            while (k > 0 && pat.charAt(i) != pat.charAt(k)) k = lps[k - 1];
            if (pat.charAt(i) == pat.charAt(k)) k++;
            lps[i] = k;
        }
        for (int i = 0, k = 0; i < text.length(); i++) {
            while (k > 0 && text.charAt(i) != pat.charAt(k)) k = lps[k - 1];
            if (text.charAt(i) == pat.charAt(k)) k++;
            if (k == pat.length()) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    // pre-order with a separator before every value and "#" for null
    private StringBuilder encode(TreeNode node, StringBuilder sb) {
        if (node == null) return sb.append(",#");
        sb.append(",^").append(node.val);
        encode(node.left, sb);
        return encode(node.right, sb);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isSubtree(self, root: Optional[TreeNode], subRoot: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        def encode(node, out):
            # pre-order with a separator before every value and "#" for null
            if not node:
                out.append("#")
                return
            out.append("^" + str(node.val))
            encode(node.left, out)
            encode(node.right, out)

        a, b = [], []
        encode(root, a)
        encode(subRoot, b)
        text, pat = ",".join(a), ",".join(b)
        # KMP search of pat in text
        lps = [0] * len(pat)
        k = 0
        for i in range(1, len(pat)):
            while k and pat[i] != pat[k]:
                k = lps[k - 1]
            if pat[i] == pat[k]:
                k += 1
            lps[i] = k
        k = 0
        for ch in text:
            while k and ch != pat[k]:
                k = lps[k - 1]
            if ch == pat[k]:
                k += 1
            if k == len(pat):
                return True
        return False
```

**Time:** O(n + m)
**Space:** O(n + m) for the strings and the KMP table
:::

**Pitfall:** Serializing without null markers or without a value separator. Then different shapes collide, and `[12]` would "contain" `[2]`.

**Key idea:** Start at the root. If both values are smaller go left, if both are larger go right; the first node where they split (or that equals one of them) is the LCA.

**Brute force:** Record the root-to-node path for `p` and for `q`, then walk both paths together and return the last node they share. Time O(h) with BST search (O(n) with a generic DFS), Space O(h) for the paths.

:::details 1. Iterative BST walk (optimal)
The BST ordering tells you which side each node is on without searching. While both targets are on the same side of the current node, step to that side. The moment they are on different sides, or one of them is the current node, no deeper node can contain both.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        TreeNode node = root;
        while (node != null) {
            if (p.val < node.val && q.val < node.val) node = node.left;
            else if (p.val > node.val && q.val > node.val) node = node.right;
            else return node; // p and q split here (or one of them is this node)
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def lowestCommonAncestor(self, root: 'TreeNode', p: 'TreeNode', q: 'TreeNode') -> 'TreeNode':
        node = root
        while node:
            if p.val < node.val and q.val < node.val:
                node = node.left
            elif p.val > node.val and q.val > node.val:
                node = node.right
            else:
                return node  # p and q split here (or one of them is this node)
        return None
```

**Time:** O(h), O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed
**Space:** O(1)
:::

:::details 2. Recursive BST walk
The same decision written recursively: recurse into the side that holds both, otherwise return the current node.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        if (p.val < root.val && q.val < root.val) return lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);
        if (p.val > root.val && q.val > root.val) return lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);
        return root;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def lowestCommonAncestor(self, root: 'TreeNode', p: 'TreeNode', q: 'TreeNode') -> 'TreeNode':
        if p.val < root.val and q.val < root.val:
            return self.lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q)
        if p.val > root.val and q.val > root.val:
            return self.lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q)
        return root
```

**Time:** O(h)
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

:::details 3. Generic binary-tree LCA (ignores ordering)
Return a node if it is `p` or `q`. Otherwise ask both subtrees: if both report something, this node is where the paths meet; else pass up whichever side found something. This is the solution for LCA in a plain binary tree (LeetCode 236). It works here but wastes the BST property.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    // works for any binary tree, ignores the BST ordering
    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        if (root == null || root == p || root == q) return root;
        TreeNode left = lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);
        TreeNode right = lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);
        if (left != null && right != null) return root;
        return left != null ? left : right;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def lowestCommonAncestor(self, root: 'TreeNode', p: 'TreeNode', q: 'TreeNode') -> 'TreeNode':
        # works for any binary tree, ignores the BST ordering
        if not root or root is p or root is q:
            return root
        left = self.lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q)
        right = self.lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q)
        if left and right:
            return root
        return left or right
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(h)
:::

**Pitfall:** Assuming `p.val < q.val`. Compare both values against the node on each side instead of ordering them.

**Key idea:** BFS with a queue; take a snapshot of the queue's size at the start of each round, and exactly that many pops make up one level.

**Brute force:** Compute the height h, then for each depth d run a fresh DFS that collects nodes at depth d. Time O(n·h), Space O(h).

:::details 1. BFS, level by level (standard)
Start with the root in a queue. Each round: read `size = len(queue)`, pop that many nodes into a new list and push their children. Children pushed during the round belong to the next level, so they are not popped yet.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        if (root != null) q.add(root);
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = q.size(); i > 0; i--) {
                TreeNode node = q.poll();
                level.add(node.val);
                if (node.left != null) q.add(node.left);
                if (node.right != null) q.add(node.right);
            }
            res.add(level);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def levelOrder(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> List[List[int]]:
        res = []
        q = deque([root] if root else [])
        while q:
            level = []
            for _ in range(len(q)):
                node = q.popleft()
                level.append(node.val)
                if node.left:
                    q.append(node.left)
                if node.right:
                    q.append(node.right)
            res.append(level)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(w), the widest level (up to about n/2), plus the output
:::

:::details 2. DFS with a depth index
Pre-order DFS, passing the depth. When you reach a depth for the first time, add an empty list; then append the value to `res[depth]`. Visiting left before right keeps each level in left-to-right order.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(root, 0, res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(TreeNode node, int depth, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (node == null) return;
        if (depth == res.size()) res.add(new ArrayList<>());
        res.get(depth).add(node.val);
        dfs(node.left, depth + 1, res);
        dfs(node.right, depth + 1, res);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def levelOrder(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> List[List[int]]:
        res = []

        def dfs(node, depth):
            if not node:
                return
            if depth == len(res):
                res.append([])
            res[depth].append(node.val)
            dfs(node.left, depth + 1)
            dfs(node.right, depth + 1)

        dfs(root, 0)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack, plus the output
:::

**Pitfall:** Using `while q` with no size snapshot. All nodes come out in BFS order, but you lose the level boundaries.

**Key idea:** The visible node at each depth is the last one in that level (BFS), or the first one reached by a DFS that visits right before left.

**Brute force:** Do a full level-order traversal into a list of lists, then take the last element of each level. Same O(n) time, but stores every level: Space O(n).

:::details 1. BFS, keep the last node of each level
Process the queue one level at a time using a size snapshot. The node popped at index `size − 1` is the rightmost on that level.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        if (root != null) q.add(root);
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int n = q.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                TreeNode node = q.poll();
                if (i == n - 1) res.add(node.val); // last node of this level
                if (node.left != null) q.add(node.left);
                if (node.right != null) q.add(node.right);
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def rightSideView(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> List[int]:
        res = []
        q = deque([root] if root else [])
        while q:
            n = len(q)
            for i in range(n):
                node = q.popleft()
                if i == n - 1:
                    res.append(node.val)  # last node of this level
                if node.left:
                    q.append(node.left)
                if node.right:
                    q.append(node.right)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(w), the widest level
:::

:::details 2. DFS, right child first
Visit right before left and pass the depth. The first node you reach at a new depth (`depth == len(res)`) is the rightmost one there; deeper nodes on the left still show up when the right side runs out.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> rightSideView(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(root, 0, res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(TreeNode node, int depth, List<Integer> res) {
        if (node == null) return;
        if (depth == res.size()) res.add(node.val); // first node seen at this depth
        dfs(node.right, depth + 1, res);
        dfs(node.left, depth + 1, res);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
